    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d709">

                <pb facs="0810" n="722" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg722"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Eddy.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-10-14"><hi rendition="#italic">October 14,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 156.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose herewith a copy of a note from
                    the Japanese minister at this capital conveying the thanks of his Government to
                    Mr. McCormick for his good offices in bringing about the return to Japan of some
                    800 Japanese subjects, who have, until lately, been remaining in Russia, and who
                    are to leave Bremen for home about the 20th instant.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>(Note.—See No. 75, October 11, 1904, from the Japanese Legation, <ref
                            target="#pg436">p. 436</ref>.)</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
